  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe central idea is to use a **simulated annealing algorithm with advanced perturbation techniques** to explore the solution space more effectively. Unlike the binary repair approach in the initial solution, we use **continuous optimization with projected gradients** to better capture the structure of the optimal solution. The algorithm takes advantage of **dual-majorization** and **periodicity patterns** to reduce the effective search space while maintaining the ability to find high-quality candidates.\n\nKey benefits:\n- Reduces dimensionality by enforcing periodicity to match the problem's translational symmetry\n- Uses continuous optimization with projection to avoid the subspace constraint issues\n- Incorporates multiple-parameterization across scales for better exploration\n- Exploits mathematical properties of the shift-maximization problem\n\nLimitations:\n- Periodicity assumption may miss truly non-periodic solutions\n- Continuous optimization may give locally-optimal solutions not realizable by binary functions\n-does not guarantee global optimality\n\nMathematical specification:\nObjective: Minimize C = max_{k} ||[0,2] h(x)(1-h_{k}(x)) dx\n\nin discrete form:\n\nWe sample 2/n space points: x_i = i * (2/n) for i=0,...,n-1\nLet h be a function from {0,...,n-1} to [0,1] with \u2211h = n/2\n\nC = (1/n) * max_{k} \u2211_{i=0}^{n-1-k} h(i) * (1 - h(i+1+k))\n\nbut note: better to use continuous correlation as in the provided evaluator.\n\nThe mathematical equivalence is maintained by the discrete nature of the samples with exact boundary handling.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - Read n points from the provided `initial_h_values` (n=600)\n   - Compute target = n/2\n   - Set initial temperature T0 = 0.02\n   - Set cooling ratio R = 0.95 (exponential cooling schedule)\n\n2. Perturbation generation:\n   We generate candidates using three methods:\n   Method 1: Single swap in a periodic context (period divides 2)\n   Method 2: Phase shifts of the periodic component\n   Method 3: Random noise injection with targeted perturbation\n\n3. For each iteration:\n   a. Select a candidate uniformly from the population\n   b. Choose a perturbation method (fixed sequence with crossover)\n   c. Apply the perturbation to generate a neighbor solution\n   d. Compute the new objective value (c5)\n   e. Apply gradient projection if move improves or by probability for larger jumps\n   f. Accept the move if:\n        - c5 improves\n        - For unimproved moves: exp(-delta/T) \u2265 random()\n   g. Update the temperature: T = T * R\n\n4. Project bound constraints:\n   For a candidate h, we ensure h_i \u2208 [0,1] and \u2211h = target.\n   The projection:\n        If violate bounds: project_individual(x) = min(1, max(0, x))\n        If imbalance: adjust_small_value(n, target-\u2211h)\n\n5. Search schedule:\n   Temperature schedule: T = T0 * R^g (after g generations)\n   Perturbation sequence cycles through three methods:\n        method1: period-preserving swap (10% budget)\n        method2: phase shift (20% budget)\n        method3: scale perturbation (70% budget)\n\n6. Local refinements:\n   After a move is accepted, use quadratic interpolation with bounds respect to shape preservation.\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n1. Periodicity enforcement: Default period divides 2 (custom parameter 'period'). Can vary period (50%, 25%, etc.)\n2. Perturbation types: Currently uses three. Can experiment with spatial blocks (periodk subsets) and multiplicative noise.\n3. Temperature schedule: Exponential (fixed R) or adaptive (T_i ~ current_best_solution metric)\n4. Elitism: Always keep the best candidate found (enabled by default)\n5. Population size: Currently 1 (single path is better than large populations here)\n\nCompute budget:\n- Cooling schedule: 5000 generations (interactions)\n- Perturbation: Fixed cost per candidate (O(n) for correlation calculation)\n- Overall complexity: O(g * n) per second. At 1300 seconds, g ~ 1000-2000 generations.\n\nFallback:\n- If time runs out, return best candidate found (even if not improved)\n- Handles dimension jumps by resampling in a projected way\n\nValidation:\n- Continuous array projection ensures bounds: project(h) = clip(h,0,1) + (target - sum(h))\n- Check objective computation: use same method as in provided compute_c5\n- Final solution: use checkerboard to ensure no numerical instability\n\nh_final, final_c5, n_points return:\n- Converted to double precision with exact bounds projection\n- sum(h) should be exactly n/2 (integer if n even)\n- Array must be binary (not continuous) since we start from binary projection\n\nSpecific implementation steps:\n1. Initialize:\n   - n = len(initial_h_values)\n   - target = n//2\n   - h_curr = np.array(initial_h_values, dtype=float)  # copy\n   - T = 0.02\n\n2. Main loop (5000 iterations):\n   - If no improvement in 3000 steps: restart with perturbed neighbor\n   - Compute current c5 = compute_c5(h_curr)\n   - For 3 perturbation attempts:\n        pert = perturbation_function(cycle=current_pert_index, n=n, period=period)\n        h_new = h_curr + perturbation_direction (noise)\n        n_r += 1\n   - If h_new not feasible: fix using projection\n   - Compute new_c5 for h_new\n   - If better: accept and reset improvement counter\n   - Else: if exp(-delta/T) \u2265 random(): accept and update if improvement\n   - T *= 0.99\n\n3. Local search every 500 steps: use quadratic bound projection\n\n4. After time or iterations complete: \n   - return h_curr, compute_c5(h_curr), n\n\nDetailed flowcharts for each operation, including:\n- Perturbation method 1: binary polar switch in cyclic subsequence (preserves period)\n- Method 2: phase shift by 1/max(1, p-1) for period p\n- Method 3: multiplicative noise (not Boolean) with shape preservation",
